17592186049132}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640781}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector To a Plane Given by Parametric Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119935 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119648} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3061cb06-f57f-57c8-8821-dbd962b1e78e" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640769}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Lines"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119936 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119649} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d11f13f0-1cfa-59ec-a04a-9483d942b1aa" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429941}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of the Plane Passing Through Three Given Points"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121049 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that a plane $\\Pi$ passes through the point $P(1,1,2)$ and that the vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle$ is perpendicular to the plane. Let's write down an equation of this plane using the dot product.\n\n![](images/0f/0f8353480099369ad92701d1799f72d46eab4bbe5c17add8cb37a4f34074311b.png)\n\nWe start by considering an arbitrary point $R(x,y,z)$ that lies on the plane. The position vectors of the points $R$ and $P$ are $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle$ respectively. So, the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =r-⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis parallel to the plane. Therefore, $\\overrightarrow{PR} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/28b6770bd681257b1030679a421b80e3f3d1e8fbb86d2060b64c7301d0878c5c.png)\n\nNow, recall that if two nonzero vectors are perpendicular, then their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PR}\\perp \\mathbf{n} \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\overrightarrow{PR}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is called the **vector equation of the plane** that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}.$ In our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle) \\cdot \\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Using the properties of the dot product, we can arrive at an alternative vector equation given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot n & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c = \\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}$ is a (scalar) constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "77d83d72-9202-50d7-af28-72062f00bfa5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3255 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121050 :tutorial/content "If we are given an equation of a plane using the dot product, we can always obtain an equation in terms of $x,$ $y,$ and $z.$ For example, suppose we have the plane that passes through the point $P(-7,5,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-6,5 \\rangle.$\n\nFirst, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -7,5,-2 \\rangle,$ and we can write down the equation of the plane using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-6,5⟩ & =⟨-7,5,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-6,5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-6,5⟩ & =-61\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-6,5⟩ & =-61 \\\\ 3x-6y+5z & =-61 \\\\ 3x-6y+5z+61 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis final equation is called the **Cartesian equation of the plane**.\n\n![](images/77/77f18981dda500889655225eabbec730b7b295e6b16cec1700d56c2e27e0ac91.png)\n\nNote that in the equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}x+({\\color{blue}-6})y+{\\color{blue}5}z+61 = 0,\n$$\n\nthe coefficients next to $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are the coordinates of the normal vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle {\\color{blue}3},{\\color{blue}-6},{\\color{blue}5} \\rangle\n$$\n\n." :tutorial/id #uuid "81332607-6152-5770-b33d-8b80c630fc8d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3256 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121051 :tutorial/content "Any plane can be uniquely determined by a point and two non-parallel direction vectors. For example, let $\\Pi$ be the plane that passes through the point $P(1,0,2)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 0,3,0 \\rangle$  and $\\mathbf{c}= \\langle -1,1,1 \\rangle.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea4ce822abec2b68d2471a2787f4e045493ca43c1c6795a7e0069de80cb5adee.png)\n\nUsing the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c},$ we can construct a **parametric equation** of the plane.\n\nFirst, let $R$ be any general point in the plane, with position vector $\\mathbf{r}.$ Notice that we can get from $P$ to $R$ using a linear combination of $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ with some (unknown for us) coefficients $s$ and $t$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b4/b40f91c3d75f092c7b29f19770364e7518d37b64ba594a311a762ad79a950e4f.png)\n\nSo, we can write the **parametric equation** of the plane as\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\mathbf{r}} = \\mathbf{p}+s{\\color{blue}\\,\\mathbf{b}}+t\\,{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{c}},\n\t\\qquad s,t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nThis formula works whenever $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are not parallel.\n\nIn our case, $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are indeed not parallel to each other. So, using column vector notation, the parametric equation for the plane $\\Pi$ is given by\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]} + s {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]} + t {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n\t\\qquad s,t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2828346e-3bca-51a8-a6ef-a86ca7034a62" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3257 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121052 :tutorial/content "Any plane can be uniquely determined by three non-collinear points. \n\n![](images/08/08e65c4e3465a4bf155dfed6bc0dc2a35bf2eb9326d9bff0c0aa75b86e97d76b.png)\n\nTo write down a parametric equation of the plane, the trick is to use the three non-collinear points to construct two displacement vectors that are parallel to the plane.\n\n![](images/16/1613ea9df76b637b93e0e4b38b70b87892140398eaa7aaccb74f2a5642124dfd.png)\n\nThen we can proceed as usual to find the equation of the plane. Let's practice this technique in the following example." :tutorial/id #uuid "e32f2dc4-38f6-541d-9dc6-9cfd1ac079eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3258 :tutorial/title "Finding the Equation of a Plane Passing Through Three Points"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121120 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a plane with the following normal vector: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -1,2,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nLet's use the scalar product to find a vector that is parallel to this plane.\n\nWe denote our parallel vector as $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle.$ Now, since $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find a nonzero solution to this equation. To do that, we solve the equation for *one* of the variables (it doesn't matter which one). For example, let's solve our equation for the variable $t_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the variable $t_1$ depends on $t_2$ and $t_3,$ which, in turn, can be *any* real numbers. The variables $t_2$ and $t_3$ are called **free variables**.\n\nHence, the general form of our vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 2t_2+t_3, \\, t_2, \\, t_3 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t_2, t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTo find a particular vector parallel to the plane, we can substitute any values for $t_2$ and $t_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- For example, setting $t_2=0$ and $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1= \\langle 1, 0, 1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Or, we could set $t_2=1$ and $t_3=0$ and get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\langle 2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n**Note:** There are infinitely many vectors parallel to a given plane. In our example, we found only two particular vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "980ef6f4-542d-5915-84b8-e34f4480ae68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6411 :tutorial/title "A Vector Parallel To a Plane"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192504} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608220}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192505} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608190}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192506} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595951}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193876 :tutorial/content "Recall that $H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nIn the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ there are two **trivial subspaces**.\n\n- One trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}\n$$\n\n consisting of just the zero vector. This is a subspace because the sum of two zero vectors is the zero vector, and any multiple of the zero vector is the zero vector.\n\n- Another trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH = \\mathbb{R}^n\n$$\n\n consisting of the entire set $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is a subspace because the sum of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ and any multiple of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "01ed4d92-df35-5f3c-aa41-43246f6ea9e9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6854 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193877 :tutorial/content "In $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ are straight lines that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows a subspace in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c0ea9584ae3229a7930c6ef2b53031770a7190b980b5b595cc233cae29ea49d.png)\n\nLet's now consider a few examples of subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that are *not* subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\n![](images/5a/5aac9808e9196653ee54974b495443a95d8913cd0b220a6893acb68ff3b967bb.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is *not* a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II is not a subspace because $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf v$ belong to the set while $\\mathbf u+\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under vector addition.\n\n- Diagram III is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $-\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." :tutorial/id #uuid "33c42a7a-a5c2-5b36-a839-4fab6351c9e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6857 :tutorial/title "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193878 :tutorial/content "In $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ are straight lines or planes that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/57/57b6625b607537d1c90e849d6ee6ac8480648aa4ad6e3a357bedc8d8408256ad.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the sets shown in the diagrams below are *not* subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4fbaee302e1c25e740f4164841a674243a43197b0f20ddb35d2d41bb4175b68.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a plane that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." :tutorial/id #uuid "8d9802f0-c392-5e37-afb4-72f73451813e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6861 :tutorial/title "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193879 :tutorial/content "Lastly, consider the set $\\mathbb{R},$ which belongs to the family of vector spaces $\\mathbb{R}^n$ (when $n=1$). This vector space has only two subspaces, both of which are trivial:\n\n- the zero subspace $\\{0\\},$ which contains only the number $0,$ and\n\n- the whole space $\\mathbb{R}$ (or, formally, $\\mathbb{R}^1$), which contains all the real numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "fb0671eb-0c5d-59dc-a3f9-cda72b4da034" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6862 :tutorial/title "Final Notes About the Set of Real Numbers"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198474 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198151} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9f0da82-f79b-5056-ac99-b401eb1630c7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049076}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649169}] :knowledge-point/title "Describing Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198475 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198163} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e694b201-5ec6-5cc3-a608-2447d1cc1ed3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050153}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383659}] :knowledge-point/title "Linear Boundaries, One Condition"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198476 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198152} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a3df69f-653e-5148-bd46-580097e43362" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050641}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383633}] :knowledge-point/title "Linear Boundaries, Two Conditions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198477 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198153} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36fa0f2f-3812-521a-aca3-900556be84ff" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049456}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433491}] :knowledge-point/title "Describing Non-Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199551 :tutorial/content "We can use set-builder notation to represent sets that lie in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, let's describe the following shaded region using set-builder-notation.\n\n![](images/2c/2c4545ab59c1bf6847ab38b09ddf8b86c3227bbc6006835aceb8637b45bff817.png)\n\n  \n\nFirst, notice that a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2<x<6,\\,2<y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that we also need to   \n\n- *include* the left edge ($x=2$), and\n\n- *include* the right edge ($x=6$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbols to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le x\\le 6,\\,2<y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb R, y\\in\\mathbb R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R = \\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{\n(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2\n\\: : \\:  \n\n2 \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq} \nx \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq}  \n6, \n\\: \n2 \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt} \ny\n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt}   \n5 \n\\Big\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "76f96423-a638-5ff2-8797-e0e8559da636" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8221 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203897 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203719} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e3b9100-91b5-59ec-8676-832893b05ebb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050276}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711496} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711558}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Cross Product Expressions Using the Distributive Laws"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203898 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203720} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88c81beb-9b15-56ae-bb46-ce5cbc1d0ea5" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711696} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711744}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Cross Product Expressions Using Anticommutativity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205256 :tutorial/content "Recall that the cross product of two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b} = | \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, | \\cdot | \\, \\mathbf{b} \\, | \\cdot \\sin\\theta \\cdot \\mathbf{n},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\theta$ is the angle formed between the vectors when their tails are placed at the same point, and\n\n- $\\mathbf{n}$ is a unit vector that's perpendicular to *both* $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nThe cross product satisfies a few properties that we would normally expect multiplication to satisfy. \n\nSpecifically, given the vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and any scalar $\\lambda,$ the following properties hold.\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition and subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples of how we can use these properties." :tutorial/id #uuid "3fea84bc-5101-54c3-998f-e4b43e8df4c6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9150 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205260 :tutorial/content "The cross product depends on the order of multiplication! In other words, the cross product is *not* commutative. \n\nMore specifically, it is **anticommutative**, meaning that if we swap the order of the multiplication, the result changes sign:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = -\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nTo understand why this is so, we need to go back to the right-hand rule for calculating $\\mathbf a \\times \\mathbf b{:}$\n\n![](images/05/058021724be36344bb806683afaa0a4243e63a3c540927388346ddb447422b4e.png)\n\n Recall that the right-hand rule says that we calculate the *direction* of the vector $\\mathbf a \\times \\mathbf b$ as follows:\n\n- First, we extend the index finger on our right hand along the direction of $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- Then, we extend our middle finger along the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- When we do this, our thumb points in the direction of $\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/42/4295a74c02deebee63d9db0f369d7f8b8d8a3956d9dcb59bd58fec19f450e07d.png)\n\nWe can also use the right-hand rule to calculate the direction of $\\mathbf b \\times \\mathbf a{:}$\n\n- First, we extend the index finger on our right hand along the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- Then, we extend our middle finger along the direction of $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- When we do this, our thumb points in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n![](images/81/818db143964d23937a18fc44e7546e5a932b87f79a5b19737833d10ed08215d9.png)\n\nSo, $\\mathbf a \\times \\mathbf b$ and $\\mathbf b \\times \\mathbf a$ have the *same* magnitude yet *opposite* direction. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a \\times \\mathbf b = -\\mathbf b \\times \\mathbf a.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "097ace06-cb20-5ce7-af56-f54d7b79669b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9156 :tutorial/title "The Anticommutativity Property"}]}
